  "text": "Solution 1 (answer 5) is the most plausible of the three, though its argument has a flaw.\n\n- **Lower bound 4 is sound.** If C ⊂ A₁×A₂ has non-constant projections, the induced map J(C) → A₁×A₂ has image projecting onto both factors. For very general A₁, A₂ the only such abelian subvariety is the whole product, so g(C) ≥ 4.\n- **Solution 2's construction fails a dimension count.** Genus-4 double covers of a genus-2 curve D (branched at 2 points) form a 5-dimensional family. Their Jacobians are Jac(D) × Prym, a 6-dimensional family of pairs of surfaces, so the family cannot dominate pairs (A₁, A₂). Its Prym-dominance claim is unjustified.\n- **Solution 1's construction has an arithmetic error.** Two genus-2 curves glued at one point give arithmetic genus 4, not 5. Gluing at two points would give genus 5. The conclusion of 5 is still plausible, and the lower-bound-4 exclusion via the very-general hypothesis is heuristic rather than proved.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 9) looks unsupported.** I could not verify the attributed Ciliberto–van der Geer result, and the description of a (2,2)-curve on Θ₁×Θ₂ does not obviously produce a curve of genus 9 with the stated minimality.\n\nI am not certain of the true value. The very-general hypothesis suggests the answer exceeds the trivial bound of 4, which favours 5 over 4.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
